00:05:20.242 --> 00:05:24.342
Motley Fool Canada walks through a tax-free savings account, or TFSA,

00:05:24.342 --> 00:05:30.322
A template that starts with $14,000 Canadian dollars and aims for $50 a month of tax-free

00:05:30.322 --> 00:05:32.462
income from a mix of dividend payers.

00:05:32.722 --> 00:05:38.202
You can replicate that allocation inside a TFSA at any major Canadian brokerage, with

00:05:38.202 --> 00:05:40.622
no extra product fee once the account is open.

00:05:41.262 --> 00:05:47.462
The screen they use wants payout ratios below 60% and five-year dividend growth above 5%,

00:05:47.462 --> 00:05:52.182
which is a concrete starting template you can tighten or loosen for your own risk tolerance.

00:05:52.182 --> 00:05:58.142
The method leans on eligible Canadian dividends so that, once the TFSA is at the contribution

00:05:58.142 --> 00:06:02.782
limit, new savings in a taxable account can still use the dividend tax credit.

00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:08.922
Because TFSA room resets every January, that same $14,000 Canadian dollar figure can

00:06:08.922 --> 00:06:13.762
be redeployed without tax friction, which is what makes the income stream repeatable rather

00:06:13.762 --> 00:06:14.862
than a one-year stunt.

